[
 {
  "identity": "note-fidelity/not-same-read/MISSING/ActiveRecord::FinderMethods#exists?/aspects.name-uniqueness/new-contact-arm",
  "source": "agent",
  "catchable": true,
  "evidence": "REAL (in/real_mode.json (real_mode_recording/1, plan amc-real-2 sha256 8715d60c…, world amc-world-2)), scenario share-with-new-local-contact AND scenario inflected-locale-reads-profile, under frame ActiveRecord::FinderMethods#exists?, inside the ActiveRecord::Base#save transaction (between BEGIN TRANSACTION and INSERT INTO \"contacts\"): `SELECT  1 AS one FROM \"aspects\" WHERE LOWER(\"aspects\".\"name\") = LOWER(?) AND \"aspects\".\"id\" != ? AND \"aspects\".\"user_id\" = ? LIMIT ?`. CORPUS (the 417 dumps under in/packet/dumps (symbolic_call events; my tabulation script is batch/notes.py, output batch/notes_by_target.json)): no note on any target reads `aspects` by name. The only aspects notes in all 417 dumps are `SELECT \"aspects\".* FROM \"aspects\" WHERE \"aspects\".\"user_id\" = $$(p0) AND \"aspects\".\"id\" = $$(p1) ORDER BY order_id ASC LIMIT 1` (FinderMethods#first) and `SELECT \"aspects\".* FROM \"aspects\" WHERE \"aspects\".\"id\" = $$(p0)` (find_target). The new-contact arm is in the corpus: 12 dumps carry `INSERT INTO \"contacts\" (...)` under ActiveRecord::Base#save, e.g. dump_auth_json_dse0007.json, so the arm was driven and this read is still missing. MECHANISM (my reading of the source; this is an argument): `contact.save` on a NEW owner validates EVERY loaded record of a validated association (activerecord-5.2.4.3 autosave_association.rb:274-276, `if new_record ... association && association.target`). That includes the persisted Aspect added by `contact.aspects << aspect` (connecting.rb:21), so Aspect's `validates_uniqueness_of :name, :scope => :user_id, :case_sensitive => false` (app/models/aspect.rb:19) issues this probe. The persisted-owner scenarios do not issue it (share-existing-receiving-contact, share-existing-nonreceiving-contact), which matches the `elsif` arms of the same method. In the concolic run ActiveRecord::Base#save is a TARGET whose mock (targets/endpoints/aspect_memberships_create.rb ~:340-367 -> core.rb dml_run_pending_autosaves!) replaces the whole save, validation chain included, so no validator runs inside it. Mechanical verdict: MISSING, which the static half treats as never declarable.",
  "consequence": "If true, this is NOT the same read. The corpus UNDERSTATES what the endpoint touches. On the new-contact arm the real request reads whether the principal owns another aspect with the same case-folded name. The shipped policy has no view over `aspects` constrained by `name`/`id !=`: its only aspects views are keyed on `id = <aspect_id param>`. An enforcing proxy built from this policy would therefore refuse a statement the real request issues on its main success path, the first share with a new person. This is not an instrument limitation, because no change to the value model is needed. A save mock that ran the owner's association validations (or emitted their probes) would produce the note. FALSIFIER: a corpus on the same config that carries an exists? note over \"aspects\" with a LOWER(name) predicate on the INSERT-contacts arm, or a real recording of share-with-new-local-contact without this statement. RULE P: share-with-new-local-contact is already in the real-mode plan (in/real_mode_plan.json `scenarios`, plan amc-real-2). With this recording mounted, checks.shape_check / statement_differential should report the class mechanically (expected as statement-shape/note-mismatch/ActiveRecord::FinderMethods#exists? or a differential on relation `aspects`). I did NOT run either check. The config change is to keep that scenario permanently."
 },
 {
  "identity": "note-fidelity/not-same-read/PROJ-DIFF/ActiveRecord::Base#save/aspect_memberships-insert-without-contact_id/new-contact-arm",
  "source": "agent",
  "catchable": true,
  "evidence": "REAL (in/real_mode.json (real_mode_recording/1, plan amc-real-2 sha256 8715d60c…, world amc-world-2)), scenario share-with-new-local-contact (also inflected-locale-reads-profile), under frame ActiveRecord::Base#save, directly after the contacts INSERT: `INSERT INTO \"aspect_memberships\" (\"aspect_id\", \"contact_id\", \"created_at\", \"updated_at\") VALUES (?, ?, ?, ?)`. CORPUS (the 417 dumps under in/packet/dumps (symbolic_call events; my tabulation script is batch/notes.py, output batch/notes_by_target.json)): on the same arm (the 12 dumps that carry INSERT INTO \"contacts\", e.g. dump_auth_json_dse0007.json), the note under ActiveRecord::Base#save (function `block in dml_run_pending_autosaves!`) is `INSERT INTO \"aspect_memberships\" (\"aspect_id\", \"created_at\", \"updated_at\") VALUES ($$(p0), '<concolic_now>', '<concolic_now>')` with refs {p0: SYM_RESULT_ActiveRecord__FinderMethods_first_1_id}. The contact_id column is absent. The same dumps then carry, under ActiveRecord::FinderMethods#first, `SELECT \"aspect_memberships\".* FROM \"aspect_memberships\" WHERE \"aspect_memberships\".\"contact_id\" IS NULL AND \"aspect_memberships\".\"aspect_id\" = $$(p0) ORDER BY \"aspect_memberships\".\"id\" ASC LIMIT 1`. The policy IR (in/policy.json, 55 atoms) contains both shapes as atoms (2 atoms each). MECHANISM (source reading): the mocked save of the new Contact returns a symbool and never assigns a primary key, so the contact's id stays nil. core.rb `dml_stamp_owner_key!` then stamps `contact_id = nil` onto the join row, and `@contact.aspect_memberships.where(aspect_id:)` renders `IS NULL`. Real ActiveRecord sets the id from the INSERT before saving the join row. Mechanical verdict: PROJ-DIFF on the write. The follow-up read is PRED-OP-DIFF (`IS NULL` vs `= ?`). The REAL side of that read is not in the recording, because this scenario ends in `Redis::CannotConnectError` at defer_dispatch before the controller's lookup runs (see the separate proposal item).",
  "consequence": "If true, the corpus records a DIFFERENT write. It says the endpoint inserts a membership row linked to no contact, when it actually inserts one linked to the newly created contact. Every atom that chains on the new contact's identity is lost: no bind connects the membership INSERT, or the post-write membership read, to the contacts INSERT. The policy's `contact_id IS NULL` read atom describes a read no real request issues. The post-write read the real request does issue (`contact_id = <new contact id>`) has no atom on this arm. Whether an enforcing proxy would admit it via the `contact_id = $$(find_by_1_id)` atom of the found-contact arm cannot be decided from what I have (that atom's condition requires the find_by FOUND arm). This is a MOCK defect, not a model limitation: the runtime can mint a symbolic id for an inserted row the same way it mints one for a found row. FALSIFIER: a real recording of the new-contact arm whose membership INSERT omits contact_id, which would contradict activerecord's has_many :through save. RULE P: share-with-new-local-contact is in the plan. With this recording mounted, shape_check should flag the INSERT's missing column if the shape comparison covers DML projections. I did not run it and do not know that it does. If it does not, the catching change is a DML-projection check, not config."
 },
 {
  "identity": "note-fidelity/not-same-read/PRED-OP-DIFF/ActiveRecord::FinderMethods#exists?/contacts.id-uniqueness-self-exclusion",
  "source": "agent",
  "catchable": true,
  "evidence": "REAL (in/real_mode.json (real_mode_recording/1, plan amc-real-2 sha256 8715d60c…, world amc-world-2)), scenarios share-existing-receiving-contact and share-existing-nonreceiving-contact, under frame ActiveRecord::FinderMethods#exists?, issued once in `contact.valid?` and again inside the ActiveRecord::Base#save transaction: `SELECT  1 AS one FROM \"contacts\" WHERE \"contacts\".\"person_id\" = ? AND \"contacts\".\"id\" != ? AND \"contacts\".\"user_id\" = ? LIMIT ?`. CORPUS (the 417 dumps under in/packet/dumps (symbolic_call events; my tabulation script is batch/notes.py, output batch/notes_by_target.json)): on the persisted-contact arm, 340 dumps carry `SELECT 1 AS one FROM \"contacts\" WHERE \"contacts\".\"person_id\" = $$(p0) AND \"contacts\".\"id\" IS NOT NULL AND \"contacts\".\"user_id\" = $$(p1) LIMIT 1` (e.g. dump_auth_json_dse0001.json, refs p0 = SYM_RESULT_ActiveRecord__FinderMethods_find_by_1_person_id, p1 = …find_by_1_user_id). No dump carries an `\"id\" != ` form. The new-record form (`person_id = ? AND user_id = ?`, 70 dumps) matches the real share-with-new-local-contact statement and is not part of this claim. MECHANISM (source reading): activerecord-5.2.4.3 validations/uniqueness.rb:24-26 adds `where.not(id: record.id_in_database)` for a persisted record. On the mock's `klass.allocate` symbolic instance, `id_in_database` is nil, while `id` is the symbolic SYM_RESULT_…_find_by_1_id. So arel renders `IS NOT NULL` and the bind is dropped. The mock's own comment at targets/endpoints/aspect_memberships_create.rb §9 (~:800-806) describes this `IS NOT NULL` as Rails behaviour for a persisted record, which it is not when the record has an id. Mechanical verdict: PRED-OP-DIFF (`!= ?` vs `IS NOT NULL`).",
  "consequence": "If true, the two notes denote DIFFERENT reads. The real probe asks whether ANOTHER contact row exists for (user, person) and excludes the record itself. The recorded one excludes nothing, so on any real database it would always match the record being validated. For the policy: the recorded view is WIDER than the real read (`IS NOT NULL` admits a superset of `!= ?`) and covers the same table, columns and principal-bound `user_id`. So I do not expect an enforcement difference from this item alone. What is wrong is the description, and the lost bind of the contact's own id. It is a MOCK defect (the symbolic id exists on the record and is simply not answered by id_in_database), not a model limitation. FALSIFIER: a real recording in which a persisted contact's uniqueness probe renders `IS NOT NULL`, which would mean the record's id_in_database really was nil. RULE P: both persisted-contact scenarios are in the plan, so shape_check over this recording should see the predicate-operator difference. I did not run it, and whether its predicate-set comparison treats `IS NOT NULL` as wider-and-acceptable is exactly what would make this uncatchable. If it does, the change needed is a PRED-OP-aware comparison, not config."
 },
 {
  "identity": "note-fidelity/overstated-write/ActiveRecord::Base#save/aspect_memberships-insert-duplicated-on-persisted-contact",
  "source": "agent",
  "catchable": false,
  "evidence": "REAL (in/real_mode.json (real_mode_recording/1, plan amc-real-2 sha256 8715d60c…, world amc-world-2)): scenario share-existing-receiving-contact issues exactly ONE membership write, `INSERT INTO \"aspect_memberships\" (\"aspect_id\", \"contact_id\", \"created_at\", \"updated_at\") VALUES (?, ?, ?, ?)` under frame ActiveRecord::Base#save!, in its own BEGIN/COMMIT (frame None). The following ActiveRecord::Base#save frame issues only `BEGIN TRANSACTION`, two exists? probes (contacts, blocks) and `COMMIT TRANSACTION`. Scenario share-existing-nonreceiving-contact is the same except that save also issues `UPDATE \"contacts\" SET \"receiving\" = ?, \"updated_at\" = ? WHERE \"contacts\".\"id\" = ?`. No real scenario issues a membership INSERT under ActiveRecord::Base#save on a persisted contact. CORPUS (the 417 dumps under in/packet/dumps (symbolic_call events; my tabulation script is batch/notes.py, output batch/notes_by_target.json)): in all 64 dumps on the persisted-contact write arm (36 with receiving==True taken false, 28 with it taken true), there is the save! INSERT `INSERT INTO \"aspect_memberships\" (\"aspect_id\", \"contact_id\", \"created_at\", \"updated_at\") VALUES ($$(p0), $$(p1), '<concolic_now>', '<concolic_now>')` AND a SECOND identical INSERT under ActiveRecord::Base#save, function `block in dml_run_pending_autosaves!` (e.g. dump_auth_json_dse0001.json, dump_auth_json_dse0016.json). MECHANISM (source reading): the save! target mock returns a symbool and never clears the through-record's new_record state. core.rb `dml_pending_autosave_records` selects records with `dml_record_is_new?(rec) == true`, so `dml_run_pending_autosaves!` (core.rb:852-882) re-saves the already-inserted join row when contact.save runs.",
  "consequence": "If true, the corpus OVERSTATES the endpoint's writes: two membership INSERTs where the real request issues one. On a real database the second would violate the join row's uniqueness. The shapes are identical, so the policy names no extra table or column, but it carries an INSERT atom under ActiveRecord::Base#save, conditioned on the persisted-contact arm, that no real request issues. A policy that is meant to describe what the endpoint does, rather than merely bound it, is wrong in multiplicity and frame. Not same read; a mock defect, and it sits in the SHARED core (core.rb dml_run_pending_autosaves!), so other write endpoints that call save! then save on the same owner may carry the same duplicate. WHY catchable=false: every built check (shape_check, statement_differential, mock_note_check) asks whether each REAL statement is covered by some note. None asks whether each NOTE has a real counterpart on the matching path. So no scenario, fixture row or must_stay_symbolic entry makes this class visible: the evidence is already in the recording and nothing reads it in that direction. Catching it needs a notes-to-real direction check, which is code shared across endpoints, hence the cross-endpoint route. FALSIFIER: a real recording of a persisted-contact share whose save frame issues the membership INSERT."
 },
 {
  "identity": "note-fidelity/overstated-write/ActiveRecord::Base#save/contacts-update-on-already-receiving-contact",
  "source": "agent",
  "catchable": false,
  "evidence": "REAL (in/real_mode.json (real_mode_recording/1, plan amc-real-2 sha256 8715d60c…, world amc-world-2)): scenario share-existing-receiving-contact (contact 100, receiving=true) issues NO write to `contacts`. Its ActiveRecord::Base#save frame is exactly `BEGIN TRANSACTION`, `SELECT  1 AS one FROM \"contacts\" WHERE \"contacts\".\"person_id\" = ? AND \"contacts\".\"id\" != ? AND \"contacts\".\"user_id\" = ? LIMIT ?`, `SELECT  1 AS one FROM \"blocks\" WHERE \"blocks\".\"user_id\" = ? AND \"blocks\".\"person_id\" = ? LIMIT ?`, `COMMIT TRANSACTION`. The real-mode plan's own description of that scenario predicts the same ('saves a clean record and writes nothing to `contacts`'). CORPUS (the 417 dumps under in/packet/dumps (symbolic_call events; my tabulation script is batch/notes.py, output batch/notes_by_target.json)): 28 dumps take the path condition `(SYM_RESULT_ActiveRecord__FinderMethods_find_by_1_receiving == True)` TRUE and still carry, under ActiveRecord::Base#save, `UPDATE \"contacts\" SET \"updated_at\" = '<concolic_now>', \"receiving\" = true WHERE \"contacts\".\"id\" = $$(p0)` (e.g. dump_auth_json_dse0016.json). MECHANISM (source reading): targets/endpoints/aspect_memberships_create.rb:499-507 re-wraps the symbolic instance's writers to append the column to `dirty` on EVERY assignment. ActiveModel::Dirty records a change only when the value differs. `share_with` assigns `contact.receiving = true` unconditionally (connecting.rb:20), so the mock reports a change where Rails sees none, and `dml_note` renders an UPDATE.",
  "consequence": "If true, the corpus OVERSTATES writes on the already-receiving arm. The policy IR carries a `contacts` UPDATE atom whose condition admits receiving==true, a state in which the real request writes nothing. On the receiving==false arm the UPDATE is real and matches share-existing-nonreceiving-contact, column order aside. That arm is not part of this claim. Not same read; a mock defect (same-value assignment treated as dirty), not a model limitation. A symbolic boolean can be compared to the assigned value as a recorded decision. WHY catchable=false: as with the duplicate-INSERT item, the evidence is an EXTRA note with no real counterpart, and no built check compares notes back to the recording, so no config change makes it fire. FALSIFIER: a real recording in which assigning receiving=true to a contact that already has receiving=true produces an UPDATE."
 },
 {
  "identity": "note-fidelity/same-read/LIMIT-DIFF/ActiveRecord::Core::ClassMethods#find+SingularAssociation#find_target/note-omits-limit",
  "source": "agent",
  "catchable": true,
  "evidence": "REAL (in/real_mode.json (real_mode_recording/1, plan amc-real-2 sha256 8715d60c…, world amc-world-2)), every scenario that reaches them: under ActiveRecord::Core::ClassMethods#find, `SELECT  \"people\".* FROM \"people\" WHERE \"people\".\"id\" = ? LIMIT ?`. Under ActiveRecord::Associations::SingularAssociation#find_target: `SELECT  \"people\".* FROM \"people\" WHERE \"people\".\"id\" = ? LIMIT ?`, `SELECT  \"users\".* FROM \"users\" WHERE \"users\".\"id\" = ? LIMIT ?`, `SELECT  \"people\".* FROM \"people\" WHERE \"people\".\"owner_id\" = ? LIMIT ?`, `SELECT  \"profiles\".* FROM \"profiles\" WHERE \"profiles\".\"person_id\" = ? LIMIT ?`, `SELECT  \"aspects\".* FROM \"aspects\" WHERE \"aspects\".\"id\" = ? LIMIT ?`. CORPUS (the 417 dumps under in/packet/dumps (symbolic_call events; my tabulation script is batch/notes.py, output batch/notes_by_target.json)): the same five statements with no LIMIT, e.g. `SELECT \"people\".* FROM \"people\" WHERE \"people\".\"id\" = $$(p0)` (find, 414 dumps) and `SELECT \"users\".* FROM \"users\" WHERE \"users\".\"id\" = $$(p0)` (find_target, 410 dumps). Tables, projection and predicates are otherwise identical. Mechanical verdict: LIMIT-DIFF (the real statement has LIMIT, the note does not), which is the inverse of the people_stream ledger entries.",
  "consequence": "If true, these are the SAME read. Each is a single-row lookup by a key column (a primary key, or a has_one foreign key that is single-valued in this app). The real LIMIT 1 is Rails' `find`/`take` bound, and the note simply does not render it. Without a LIMIT the note is a superset of the real read, so the policy's views over people/users/profiles/aspects are correct in tables, predicates and binds, and are at most wider by the LIMIT. This is a rendering difference in the find/find_target mocks' notes, not a preload-arity limitation, and not a defect that moves any policy atom. FALSIFIER: a real recording in which one of these frames issues the read with no LIMIT, or with LIMIT > 1, or a has_one reached here that is NOT single-valued (`people.owner_id` and `profiles.person_id` are the two to check). RULE P: this is the shape of a note_fidelity_declared entry (statement + LIMIT-DIFF). The config change is to declare exactly these five (statement, LIMIT-DIFF) pairs, wherever this config's completion block keeps declared note-fidelity exemptions (completion_config.json `note_fidelity_declared` in the results3 layout; I did not find the equivalent key in in/config.json and have not assumed it)."
 },
 {
  "identity": "proposal/real-mode-plan/needs-dispatch-arms-must-reach-past-defer_dispatch",
  "source": "agent",
  "catchable": true,
  "evidence": "REAL (in/real_mode.json (real_mode_recording/1, plan amc-real-2 sha256 8715d60c…, world amc-world-2)): three scenarios end in `Redis::CannotConnectError: Error connecting to Redis on 127.0.0.1:6379 (Errno::ECONNREFUSED)`: share-with-new-local-contact, inflected-locale-reads-profile and share-existing-nonreceiving-contact. Their last recorded statement is COMMIT of contact.save. So on every needs_dispatch arm the recording has neither `UPDATE \"notifications\" ... ` (update_all) nor the controller's post-write `aspect_memberships` lookup and its aspects find_target. The plan's `_why_scenario_2` anticipates this for the notifications UPDATE and covers it with share-existing-receiving-contact (needs_dispatch=false). But the post-write membership read on the NEW-contact arm (corpus: `... \"aspect_memberships\".\"contact_id\" IS NULL AND \"aspect_memberships\".\"aspect_id\" = $$(p0) ...`, 12 dumps) has no real counterpart in any scenario. That is why the PROJ-DIFF item above can decide the INSERT but not the read.",
  "consequence": "If accepted, the needs_dispatch arms get recorded to the end of the action, and the new-contact post-write read becomes mechanically comparable (shape_check) instead of undecidable. PROPOSAL, concrete enough to apply, NOT applied: in in/real_mode_plan.json (plan amc-real-2, file local_amc3/plan/real_mode_plan.json), make the Sidekiq push succeed without touching app code, by one of: (a) a Redis reachable at 127.0.0.1:6379 in the real-mode environment, or (b) a framework-level testing mode (`Sidekiq::Testing.fake!`) selected by a rig key, since the plan's `rig` block is where framework stand-ins are declared. No such key exists today; I would name it `rig.sidekiq_mode: \"fake\"` and note that the probe (domains/ruby_rails_sql/real_mode/real_mode_probe.rb) would have to honour it. Expected new statements in share-with-new-local-contact after COMMIT: `UPDATE \"notifications\" SET \"unread\" = 'f' WHERE \"notifications\".\"type\" IN ('Notifications::StartedSharing') AND \"notifications\".\"recipient_id\" = ? AND \"notifications\".\"target_id\" = ? AND \"notifications\".\"unread\" = ?` under ActiveRecord::Relation#update_all, then `SELECT  \"aspect_memberships\".* FROM \"aspect_memberships\" WHERE \"aspect_memberships\".\"contact_id\" = ? AND \"aspect_memberships\".\"aspect_id\" = ? ORDER BY \"aspect_memberships\".\"id\" ASC LIMIT ?` under ActiveRecord::FinderMethods#first. The second is my prediction from activerecord's has_many scoping, not a measurement. The plan's stated principle is that the probe never stubs app classes. (a) respects it fully; (b) stubs a framework client, not an app class, and the coordinator must judge whether that keeps the recording ground truth. I am not deciding that."
 }
]